problems.solution:
`week2/generated/day3-practice-unique.spec.ts`:

```ts
import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test";

test("a fresh first name on every run", async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto("https://demo.automationtesting.in/Register.html");

  const firstName = "Learner" + Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 8);
  console.log("generated:", firstName);

  const field = page.getByPlaceholder("First Name");
  await field.fill(firstName);

  await expect(field).toHaveValue(firstName);
});
```

Run it twice and compare the two `generated:` lines — different every time.

**Why the value goes in a variable first.** The temptation is to inline the expression into `fill()`. Do that and the assertion has nothing to compare against: `Math.random()` gives a *different* string the second time you call it, so the test would assert the field holds a value it was never given. Generate once, hold it, use it in both places.

**Why unique data at all.** It is the fix the course chose over the three obvious alternatives — delete-the-record afterwards (leaves the suite broken if it fails mid-way), a file of ten prepared names (runs out on the eleventh run), and a timestamp (collides when two tests start in the same second). A random suffix makes the duplicate check simply unreachable.

> `Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 8)` gives six alphanumeric characters. `Math.floor(Math.random() * 100000)` is equally fine where the field only accepts digits.

