problems.solution:
```ts
import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test";

test("pick a real option at random and prove it exists before selecting it", async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto("https://demo.automationtesting.in/Register.html");

  // Read the options the page ACTUALLY has - never guess at them.
  const values = await page.locator("#Skills option").evaluateAll(
    (options) => options.map((o) => (o as HTMLOptionElement).value),
  );
  console.log("option count:", values.length);

  const chosen = values[Math.floor(Math.random() * values.length)];
  console.log("chose:", chosen);

  // Build the locator from the choice, then prove it before acting on it.
  const optionXPath = "//option[@value='" + chosen + "']";
  await expect(page.locator("xpath=" + optionXPath)).toHaveCount(1);

  await page.locator("#Skills").selectOption(chosen);
  await expect(page.locator("#Skills")).toHaveValue(chosen);
});
```

**The `toHaveCount(1)` step is the whole exercise.** A dynamic locator is a string you built at runtime, and a string you built can be wrong — a stray space, an apostrophe in the value, an option that was not there today. Asserting the count *before* you act turns a confusing "element not found, waited 30 s" into an immediate, readable "expected 1, got 0".

It also guards a nastier failure: if your expression accidentally matched **two** options, `selectOption` would quietly take one of them and the test would pass while testing something you did not intend.

**Reading the options rather than hard-coding them** is the other half. `#Skills` carries 78 of them; a list you typed out by hand is wrong the day someone edits the page.

> Run it several times. Different option each run, still passing — that is the property you want from dynamic data.

