problems.solution:
Add the generator to `week2/generated/registration-helpers.ts`:

```ts
/** Digits only - the tel field rejects anything else. */
export function uniquePhone(): string {
  return "555" + Math.floor(Math.random() * 10_000_000).toString().padStart(7, "0");
}
```

Then a spec that fills the form twice and asserts both values:

```ts
import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test";
import { fillRegistrationForm, uniquePhone } from "./registration-helpers";

test("two runs of the same form get two different phone numbers", async ({ page }) => {
  const phoneOne = uniquePhone();
  await fillRegistrationForm(page, "learner.one@example.com");
  await page.locator("input[type='tel']").fill(phoneOne);
  await expect(page.locator("input[type='tel']")).toHaveValue(phoneOne);

  const phoneTwo = uniquePhone();
  await fillRegistrationForm(page, "learner.two@example.com"); // re-navigates, clearing the form
  await page.locator("input[type='tel']").fill(phoneTwo);
  await expect(page.locator("input[type='tel']")).toHaveValue(phoneTwo);

  expect(phoneOne).not.toBe(phoneTwo);
});
```

**`padStart` is doing real work.** Without it, `Math.floor(Math.random() * 10_000_000)` occasionally produces a short number, and you get a six-digit phone where the test expected ten. A generator that is *usually* the right shape is the kind of flakiness that appears once a fortnight and is miserable to chase.

**Why no `@example.com` suffix.** The email generator's suffix makes a valid address; a `tel` field rejects it. Each generator's rule comes from the field it feeds, which is why this is a separate function rather than a parameter on the email one.

**The final `expect(phoneOne).not.toBe(phoneTwo)`** asserts the generator's actual contract. Without it the test still passes if `uniquePhone()` returns a constant — and a "unique" generator that is not unique is exactly the bug worth catching.
